      "math_academy_id": "q-264791",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring",
      "problem": "In **ascending order,** the solutions of the equation $3{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x}+6\\cdot {7}^{x}=-11x\\cdot {7}^{x}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x}+6\\cdot {7}^{x} & =-11x\\cdot {7}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x}+11x\\cdot {7}^{x}+6\\cdot {7}^{x}+ & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+11x+6)\\cdot {7}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+11x+6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}+11x+6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+2)(x+3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n- The equation ${7}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the original equation has the solutions \n\n$$\nx=-3\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
