      "math_academy_id": "q-100797",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8aaf12cf-8596-5960-bc68-5ca3eb5fab0f",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring",
      "problem": "What is the product of solutions of the equation $5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x}=13x\\cdot {8}^{x}+6\\cdot {8}^{x}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x} & =13x\\cdot {8}^{x}+6\\cdot {8}^{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x}-13x\\cdot {8}^{x}-6\\cdot {8}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (5{x}^{2}-13x-6)\\cdot {8}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-13x-6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{8}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $5{x}^{2}-13x-6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(5x+2)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{2}{5},3.$\n\n- The equation ${8}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${8}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{2}{5},3,$ and their product is $(-\\frac{2}{5})\\cdot 3=-\\frac{6}{5}.$",
