      "math_academy_id": "e-7836",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3758cf02-ba4b-539a-bc61-8c2030ff6f6f",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring",
      "problem": "What is the product of solutions of the equation $x^2e^{x-1} - 2x e^{x-1} -15 e^{x-1}  = 0?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x-1}-2x{e}^{x-1}-15{e}^{x-1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x-15){e}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 15 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad e^{x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x^2 - 2x - 15  = 0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5.$\n\n- The equation $e^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because $e^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5,$ and their product is $(-3) \\cdot 5 = -15.$",
