      "knowledge_point": "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring",
      "problem": "What is the product of solutions of the equation $9x^2\\cdot 3^{x+1} = 16\\cdot 3^{x+1}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1}-16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-16){3}^{x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9x^2 - 16  = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^{x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9x^2 - 16=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+4)(3x-4) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\n- The equation $3^{x+1}=0$ has no solutions because $3^{x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3},$ and their product is $\\left( -\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{3} = -\\dfrac{16}{9}.$",
